\[ \text{Population six years ago} = 3000 \times (N - 6) \]
\[ \text{Population six years ago} = 3000 \times 23 = 69000 \]

Since the population of Chile six years ago was 69000, and it is half of the current population, we can find the current population by doubling the population six years ago:
\[ \text{Current population} = 2 \times 69000 = 138000 \]
